The friend has a point. I did say the trousers thing was what changed my habits, and that was about how I looked, not a number on a packet. Thirty-odd pence difference at the machine matters more to me than any badge, and a warning that makes me feel like I'm being lectured while I'm grabbing my tins is just irritating.

Looking at it again, the calorie badge still gives me something quick to compare, but I'd only use it if the lower-calorie option were close on price, which it usually isn't. The fear messages probably do sink in a bit for someone already anxious about money, so I won't rule them out, but they'd still bounce off me. The one I'd least mind seeing is the plain snacks with nothing added, because it doesn't tell me off.

{"points": {"A": 35, "B": 10, "C": 25, "D": 5, "E": 15, "F": 10}, "most_ignored": "D", "why": "A graphic medical photo on a packet reads as nagging and I'd probably scroll past it, while plain packaging at least doesn't make me feel judged over a twelve-pound week that the labelling doesn't change anyway."}

My gut says the friend is right about price. On the till I see people grab whatever's reduced, and I'd be doing the same. A calorie number might catch my eye on a break, but it wouldn't stop me buying a sausage roll or a Dairy Milk if it's cheaper.

I'm still sceptical of the warnings. A bowel cancer line would just make me roll my eyes, and the pictures would only make me feel worse about the Doritos I already bought. But I'd admit the badge is still a bit of "you're overdoing it" in a different font, so I've put less weight on it than before.

